---
description: Use correct semantic HTML for interactive controls — especially buttons and links
alwaysApply: true
---

# Semantic HTML

Use the right element for each job. Do not fake controls with styled `<div>` or `<span>` elements.

## Buttons

- Use `<button type="button">` for actions that stay on the page (toggle, submit a form, open a dialog, run in-page logic).
- Use `<button type="submit">` only inside a `<form>`.
- Reset button styles in CSS when needed (`appearance: none`, `border: none`, `font: inherit`, `cursor: pointer`).
- Do not nest a `<button>` inside an `<a>` — that is invalid HTML.

```html
<!-- Bad -->
<a href="/page/">
  <span class="btn">View</span>
</a>

<!-- Good: navigation -->
<a class="dd-project-card" href="/page/">View project</a>

<!-- Good: action or navigation via script on a card shell -->
<article class="dd-project-card--linked" data-project-href="/page/" tabindex="0">
  <p>Description</p>
  <button type="button" class="dd-project-card__view">View</button>
</article>
```

## Links

- Use `<a href="...">` for navigation to another URL, page, or in-site route.
- Do not use `<button>` or `<div onclick="location.href=...">` when a plain link is enough and no extra card-click behavior is required.

## Labels

- Interactive controls need accessible names: visible text, `aria-label`, or associated `<label>`.
- Write button and link labels in sentence case or title case — never all caps (see no-all-caps rule).
